arina of time  when the hero of time link and zelda and  ganondorf were done with all their drama  ganondorf gets sealed temporarily and  zelda sends link back in time by the  span of seven years this action splits  the timeline into two one following the  zelda that still stuck seven years in  the future called the adult timeline and  the other following the hero of time as  he returned to his childhood seven years  in the past called the child timeline  still with me okay good because this is  where the sauce thickens see there's  also another timeline that branches out  of ocarina of time in addition to the  child and adult ones the fallen timeline  this happens if ganondorf is able to  kill link and that's it no further  explanation don't know when or how but  that's just it that's the other timeline  yep these three timelines adult child  and fallen each extend for a few games  and then just kind of stop and then  breath of the wild takes place after all  of them but not connected to any one of  them so that's the briefest timeline  explanation of the zelda universe in the  world you're welcome now let's start  talking about what doesn't make sense  really there are just three big reasons  why the timeline is so problematic but  let's start with the latest and work  backwards in the first place breath of  the wild's timeline placement is  problematic and if anything gives more  credence to those who would like to  insinuate that the zelda universe  actually doesn't have a timeline but  rather that every game is simply its own  event with no connections to any other  games i disagree with this train of  thought personally since the wind waker  intro kind of blows that entire argument  out of the water when it just so happens  to perfectly describe the events of  ocarina of time's adult timeline ending  even down to saying the hero of time  popped in and out of time itself which  hey just so happens to be exactly what  happened proving that the games do take  place at different points of time and  can and do reference one another this  being just one example and in 2011 the  hyrule historia book was published which  featured the first publicly released  licensed by nintendo official canon  zelda timeline and if you're wondering  if we can really trust the hyrule  historio or any of the dark horse zelda  books check out this video by my good  buddy monster maze in my opinion the  existence of the zelda timeline as a  whole is really not debatable although  it will eternally be debated since  headcanon is a very strong thing but  anyway back to breath of the wild by the  time it released in 2017 it was clear  that aonuma for some reason decided to  be very very vague with its timeline  placement only confirming that it's the  most futuristic zelda game to date even  the in-game evidence for which timeline  it's supposed to take place in is super  vague there are references to all of the  timelines zelda mentions the twilight of  the child timeline there are koroks and  sea salt from the adult timeline and  there are lynels in the location of the  master sword and the lost woods which  only occurs this way in the fallen  timeline with breath of the wild being  so annoyingly vague it's also spawned  countless timeline placement theories  all across youtube some of which i've  even made as i'm sure my veteran viewers  will recall but let's face it the fact  that breath of the wild itself appears  to reference all three while not being  connected to any one of them also makes  one feel that the timeline either won  had some huge merging event happen like  in hyrule wars um or two that the  timeline doesn't actually matter this is  reason number one for why the zelda  timeline is impossible reason number two  i'm not sure if anyone remembers this  but link's awakening used to take place  after the oracle games in the downfall  timeline it was a nice way of wrapping  up the adventure of the link from link  to the past with link's a